warehouse_sk,i_item_sk,7\n       ,stdev,mean, case mean when 0 then null else stdev/mean end cov\n from(select w_warehouse_name,w_warehouse_sk,i_item_sk,7\n            ,stddev_samp(inv_quantity_on_hand) stdev,avg(inv_quantity_on_hand) mean\n      from inventory\n          ,item\n          ,warehouse\n          ,date_dim\n      where inv_item_sk = i_item_sk\n        and inv_warehouse_sk = w_warehouse_sk\n        and inv_date_sk = d_date_sk\n        and d_year =1999\n        and i_category IN (\'Books\', \'Shoes\')\n        and i_manager_id BETWEEN 81 and 100\n        and inv_quantity_on_hand between 800 and 1000\n      group by w_warehouse_name,w_warehouse_sk,i_item_sk,7) foo\n where case mean when 0 then 0 else stdev/mean end > 1)\nselect inv1.w_warehouse_sk,inv1.i_item_sk,7,inv1.mean, inv1.cov\n        ,inv2.w_warehouse_sk,inv2.i_item_sk,8,inv2.mean, inv2.cov\nfrom inv inv1,inv inv2\nwhere inv1.i_item_sk = inv2.i_item_sk\n  and inv1.w_warehouse_sk =  inv2.w_warehouse_sk\n  and inv1.d_moy=7\n  and inv2.d_moy=8\norder by inv1.w_warehouse_sk,inv1.i_item_sk,inv1.mean,inv1.cov\n        ,inv2.d_moy,inv2.mean, inv2.cov;\n```"""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""According to Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, we can apply the following transformations:\n\n- Directly apply the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection.\n- Eliminate the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nIn this case, the query rewrite rule applies these transformations to the given SQL query. The rewritten query will have the same logical plan as the original query, but with the aggregate functions and `GROUP BY` clause applied directly to the data source in the inner query."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The SQL query changes can be explained as follows:\n\nThe original query had a subquery with a complex WHERE clause that checked if the ratio of standard deviation to mean was greater than 1. This condition was rewritten using logical rules and known constraints, resulting in a simpler and more efficient condition. Specifically, the rewritten condition checks if the mean is not equal to 0 and then calculates the ratio of standard deviation to mean. If the result is greater than 1, it returns true; otherwise, it returns false.\n\nThe rewritten query has the same logical meaning as the original query but with a simpler and more efficient WHERE clause."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""The original query can be rewritten as follows:\n\n```sql\nSELECT k.killerID, COUNT(k.killerID) AS tot_kills, p.Username\nFROM kills k\nINNER JOIN players p ON k.killerid = p.id\nWHERE killText LIKE \'%$gun%\'\nGROUP BY k.killerID\nORDER BY tot_kills DESC;\n```\n\nThis rewritten query uses a single INNER JOIN to combine the `kills` and `players` tables based on the `killerid` column, eliminating the need for separate queries."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""The original script can be rewritten as follows:\n\n```sql\nWITH alphas AS (\n    SELECT v.ch\n    FROM (VALUES (\'A\'), (\'B\'), . . . ) v(ch)\n),\ndigits AS (\n    SELECT v.ch\n    FROM (VALUES (\'0\'), (\'1\'), . . . ) v(ch)\n)\nSELECT (a1.ch + a2.ch + a3.ch + d1.ch + d2.ch + d3.ch + d4.ch) as code\nFROM alphas a1 CROSS JOIN\n     alphas a2 CROSS JOIN\n     alphas a3 CROSS JOIN\n     digits d1 CROSS JOIN\n     digits d2 CROSS JOIN\n     digits d3 CROSS JOIN\n     digits d4;\n```\n\nThis rewritten query uses a combination of Common Table Expressions (CTEs) and CROSS JOINs to generate the AlphaNumeric codes, eliminating the need for the WHILE loop."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""The original query can be rewritten as follows:\n\n```sql\nWITH tempA AS (\n    SELECT P.ProjectID, A.Number\n    FROM Project AS P\n    LEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateA) AS A ON P.ProjectID = A.ProjectID\n),\ntempB AS (\n    SELECT P.ProjectID, B.Number\n    FROM Project AS P\n    LEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateB) AS B ON P.ProjectID = B.ProjectID\n)\nSELECT P.ProjectName, A.Number, B.Number\nFROM Project AS P\nLEFT JOIN tempA AS A ON P.ProjectID = A.ProjectID\nLEFT JOIN tempB AS B ON P.ProjectID = B.ProjectID;\n```\n\nThis rewritten query uses two CTEs to store the results of the separate table function joins, eliminating the need for multiple joins in a single query."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""The original query can be rewritten as follows:\n\n```sql\nPREPARE stmt FROM \'SELECT thing FROM table WHERE id=?\';\nEXECUTE stmt USING $id;\n```\n\nThis rewritten query uses prepared statements to improve security and performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 10:\n"""The original query can be rewritten as follows:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM table WHERE status = \'Discontinued\';\n```\n\nThis rewritten query uses the `=` operator to compare the `status` column with the desired value, eliminating the need for LIKE or IN operators.\n\nNote that these are just proposed query rewrite approaches and may not necessarily improve performance or security. The actual impact of rewriting a query will depend on the specific use case and database system being used."""'}], 'model': 'llama3:8b', 'max_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
